  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: This component fails basic accessibility requirements for interactive widgets. It uses a non-interactive semantic element (`<div>`) to drive behavior, completely omitting keyboard activation, focus management, and programmatic state communication to assistive technology. Screen reader users receive no indication that the title is interactive or its expanded state, while keyboard users cannot reach or activate it at all. The design requires immediate remediation before implementation.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\nBased on the component type (Disclosure/Expandable Section) and the explicit title \"Using Div Instead of Button\", I predicted:\n1. Missing keyboard activation (`Enter`/`Space`) due to `<div>` usage.\n2. Missing ARIA states (`aria-expanded`, `role=\"button\"`) causing AT blindness.\n3. Focus completely absent from tab order.\n4. Visual state indicators (`+`/`\u2212`) announced confusingly to screen readers without proper hiding.\n*Investigation confirmed all predictions.*\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Non-interactive element used for behavior; missing keyboard activation & focus management.**\n   - Evidence: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:10-12` (`<div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>`)\n   - User Group: Keyboard-only users, Screen reader users\n   - Expected Behavior: Per WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard) and WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern, interactive triggers must be native `<button>` elements or have `role=\"button\"` + `tabIndex={0}` + keyboard event handlers (`onKeyDown` for Enter/Space).\n   - Fix: Replace `<div>` with `<button type=\"button\">`. Remove `onClick`, add `onKeyDown={(e) => e.key === 'Enter' && toggle()}`. Add visible focus styles (e.g., `outline: 2px solid`).\n\n2. **Expanded state not communicated to assistive technology.**\n   - Evidence: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:10` (trigger div lacks ARIA) & `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:14` (`{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}`)\n   - User Group: Screen reader users\n   - Expected Behavior: Per WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), the trigger must expose its state via `aria-expanded={isExpanded}`. The content panel must be referenced via `id` on the panel and `aria-controls` on the button.\n   - Fix: Add `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` to the trigger. Assign a unique `id` (e.g., `content-1`) to the expandable div and add `aria-controls=\"content-1\"`.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Visual state symbols (`+`/`\u2212`) exposed to assistive technology.**\n   - Evidence: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:14` (`{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}`)\n   - User Group: Screen reader users\n   - Expected Behavior: Per WCAG 4.1.2, decorative or redundant visual indicators should be hidden from AT when state is communicated programmatically. Otherwise, SR users hear \"title, plus\" or \"title, minus\", which is confusing and redundant if `aria-expanded` is present.\n   - Fix: Wrap the symbols in `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}</span>` once ARIA states are added.\n\n2. **Missing association between trigger and content panel.**\n   - Evidence: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:16-19` (content div lacks `id`)\n   - User Group: Screen reader users, Cognitive users\n   - Expected Behavior: Per WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern, the relationship between the button and the region it controls must be explicit via `aria-controls`. This allows SR users to navigate directly to the panel or understand the structural relationship.\n   - Fix: Add `id=\"expandable-content-{uniqueId}\"` to the content `<div>` and sync it with `aria-controls` on the trigger.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **No visible focus indicator defined in component scope.** While CSS isn't provided, interactive elements must have a visible focus ring meeting WCAG 2.4.7 (Focus Visible) and 2.4.13 (Focus Appearance). Ensure `:focus-visible` styles are applied to the trigger with at least 3:1 contrast change against adjacent colors.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- **Consider native `<details>/<summary>`**: If this is a simple expand/collapse without complex state management, HTML5 `<details><summary>` provides built-in keyboard support, ARIA states, and focus management with zero JavaScript. Only use custom `<button>` + ARIA if framework-specific state or animation requirements demand it.\n- **Add `aria-label` or visible text context**: If the title alone doesn't convey the action, ensure the button's accessible name clearly indicates its purpose (e.g., \"Expand section: Title\" or rely on the visible title text which will be announced as the button label).\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- Missing `role=\"button\"` or native `<button>` semantics.\n- Missing `aria-expanded` state binding.\n- Missing `aria-controls` pairing between trigger and panel.\n- Missing keyboard event handlers (`onKeyDown`) for Enter/Space activation.\n- Missing `tabIndex={0}` (if div is kept, though it shouldn't be).\n- Missing `aria-hidden=\"true\"` on the `+`/`\u2212` visual indicators.\n- Missing focus-visible styling rules.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal & Impact |\n|-------------|-------------|-------------------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | Hears raw text without knowing it's a button or its state. No structural relationship to content. |\n| Keyboard-only | HIGH | Element is not in the tab order (`tabIndex` missing). Cannot activate with Enter/Space. Complete access loss. |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Relies on visual cues (+/-) that AT cannot convey. Focus ring likely absent or insufficient. |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Interaction model is ambiguous without standard button semantics and keyboard affordances. |\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component violates foundational accessibility principles for interactive widgets. It masks interactive intent behind a generic `<div>`, omits all required ARIA states for the Disclosure pattern, and provides zero keyboard support. These are not minor styling or labeling issues; they represent complete access barriers for keyboard and screen reader users. The design must be revised to use native semantics (`<button>` or `<details>`) and implement the complete WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern before implementation proceeds.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Is there a specific framework requirement preventing the use of native `<button>` or `<details>/<summary>`? If so, document why custom ARIA is necessary.\n- How will unique IDs be generated for `aria-controls`/`id` pairing to avoid DOM collisions if multiple instances render on one page? (Recommend using a component-level counter or UUID generator).",
